WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.701
The role of the product manager has
changed dramatically in the last few

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:07.570
years, traditionally it was
just focus on execution,

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:09.966
making sure that the train
is running on time.

00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:13.887
Now, the product manager has become
the mini-CEO of the product.

00:00:13.887 --> 00:00:18.493
[MUSIC]

00:00:18.493 --> 00:00:21.618
&gt;&gt; The Chief Product Officer Role is
a recent invention to recent development

00:00:21.618 --> 00:00:24.891
that we're seeing, but this is where
businesses are going we will see more and

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:27.338
more chief product officers and
more and more companies.

00:00:27.338 --> 00:00:30.863
And we'll see more and
more people who are growing up and

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:35.674
they want to be product people, and
we're here to help define what that is.

00:00:35.674 --> 00:00:40.308
&gt;&gt; The product manager today needs to be
good at strategy, not just product product

00:00:40.308 --> 00:00:44.957
strategy, but also corporate strategy and
making sure that they're aligned.

00:00:44.957 --> 00:00:49.168
Making sure that the customer gets
the most positive experience,

00:00:49.168 --> 00:00:53.829
the product manager needs to get very
deep level insights about a customer

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:56.625
at various stages of the customer journey.

00:00:56.625 --> 00:00:59.508
The product manager has to be
effective at decision shaping,

00:00:59.508 --> 00:01:00.843
shaping others decisions.

00:01:00.843 --> 00:01:01.474
[MUSIC]

00:01:01.474 --> 00:01:05.918
Especially of key stakeholders
within one's own organization,

00:01:05.918 --> 00:01:07.913
many of whom are risk averse.

00:01:07.913 --> 00:01:12.151
It's the first program targeted at
emerging chief product officers with a lot

00:01:12.151 --> 00:01:14.143
of product management experience,

00:01:14.143 --> 00:01:17.562
who have their eyes set on evolving
into the C Suite executive.

00:01:17.562 --> 00:01:21.163
And the program is intended to
provide the right kind of skill sets,

00:01:21.163 --> 00:01:25.397
the right kind of framework, the right
kind of real-world experiences to make

00:01:25.397 --> 00:01:29.149
sure that they become more effective
as they merge into the C-suite.

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:32.073
&gt;&gt; Our program has both
an in-class component, but

00:01:32.073 --> 00:01:36.877
also an out-of-class component that forces
our participants to go out there and

00:01:36.877 --> 00:01:41.192
actively listen to customers in the
context of a workshop and come back and

00:01:41.192 --> 00:01:45.527
actually create products based on
that listening process to customers.

00:01:45.527 --> 00:01:48.707
&gt;&gt; Let us not ignore the fact that
we are in Silicon Valley, and

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:52.723
they will get a chance to get exposed
to the Silicon Valley mindset as well.

00:01:52.723 --> 00:01:56.183
&gt;&gt; The Chief Product officer is not
simply managing the product, but

00:01:56.183 --> 00:02:00.449
it's also coordinating the development and
the management of the product within

00:02:00.449 --> 00:02:02.889
the organization all
the way up to the C suite.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:07.206
This is not just a product manager but
it's really a product leader, this program

00:02:07.206 --> 00:02:11.226
is intended for those people who want to
be the product leaders in their firm.

00:02:11.226 --> 00:02:14.326
&gt;&gt; It is a long term relationship
that we are trying to create,

00:02:14.326 --> 00:02:18.666
with future leaders in the world, who want
to make the difference, I mean all we can

00:02:18.666 --> 00:02:22.887
do is provide the spark, it is the leaders
who want to go there and have the impact.

